<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.6.3: Exploring Related Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00154</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.6.3: Exploring Related Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>93418012-ce14-4f37-9d9c-55a2b8ef6b59</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>With a partner, discuss the following situations and equations. For each, consider:</para>
<list>
  <item>
    What does the solution mean in the context of the situation?
  </item>
  <item>
    Are the given values solutions?
  </item>
</list>
<para>If operations are applied correctly, the solution to an equation is also the solution to all equations equivalent to
  it. If operations are incorrectly applied, the solution of each equation is different.</para>
<para>For numbers 1 and 2, use the following situation and equation:</para>
<para>Noah is buying a pair of jeans and using a coupon for 10% off. The total price is $56.70, which includes $2.70 in
  sales tax. Noah’s purchase can be modeled by the equation:</para>
<para>\(x−0.1x+2.70=56.70\)</para>
<!--Q1-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        What does the solution to the equation mean in this situation?
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      The solution is the price of a pair of jeans.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q2-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        How can you verify that 70 is not a solution but 60 is the solution?
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
    
    <para>70 can be substituted into the equation to make a false statement:
      \(70−0.1(70)+2.70=56.70\)
      \(65.70 ≠ 56.70\) ❌</para>
    
    <para>60 can be substituted into the equation to make a true statement:
      \(
      60−0.1(60)+2.70=56.70\)
      \(56.70=56.70\) \(\checkmark\) </para>
    
    <para>Here are some equations that are related to \(x−0.1x+2.70=56.70\). Each equation is a result of performing one or
      more moves on that original equation. Each can also be interpreted in terms of Noah’s purchase.</para>
    
    <para>For numbers 3 – 5, use the equation \(100x−10x+270=5,670\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q3-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        What was done to Noah’s equation to make this one?
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      Every term was multiplied by 100. The equation is equivalent to Noah’s equation.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q4-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        What is the interpretation of this new equation?
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      The price is now expressed in cents instead of dollars.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q5-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        Is the solution the same?
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      60 is a solution in both equations.</para>
    
    <para>For numbers 6 – 8, use the equation \(x−0.1x=54\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q6-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        What was done to Noah’s equation to make this one?
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      Subtract 2.70 from both sides of the equation. The equation is equivalent to Noah’s equation.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q7-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        What is the interpretation of this new equation?
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      The price is still in dollars.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q8-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        Is the solution the same?
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      60 is a solution in both equations.</para>
    
    <para>For numbers 9 – 11, use the equation \(0.9x+2.70=56.70\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q9-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        What was done to Noah’s equation to make this one?
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      The terms \(x\) and 0.1x were correctly combined to create an equivalent equation to Noah’s original equation.
    </para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q10-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        What is the interpretation of this new equation?
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    
    <para>10% off means paying 90% of the original price. 90% of the original price plus sales tax is $56.70.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q11-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        Is the solution the same?
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    
    <para>60 is a solution in both equations.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para>For numbers 12 – 14, use the equation \(x−0.1x=56.70\).</para>

<!--Q12-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        What was done to Noah’s equation to make this one?
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
  
  <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    
    <para>Sales tax was not included on the left but was included on the right. This equation is not equivalent to Noah’s
      original equation.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q13-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        What is the interpretation of this new equation?
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
  
  <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    
    <para>The price after using the coupon for 10% off before sales tax is not the same as the price after using the coupon for
      10% off including the sales tax, $56.70.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q14-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        Is the solution the same?
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
