  <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    
    <para>60 is not a solution to this equation.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para>For numbers 15–17, use the equation \(x−0.1x=59.40\)</para>

<!--Q15-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        What was done to Noah’s equation to make this one?
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
  
  <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      Subtract 2.70 from the left and add 2.70 to the right.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q16-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        What is the interpretation of this new equation?
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
  
  <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    
    <para>The same operation must be done to both sides of an equation to keep it balanced and to create an equivalent
      equation. The price after using the coupon for 10% off is not equal to 56.70 plus tax.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q17-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        Is the solution the same?
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
  
  <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      60 is not a solution to this equation.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para>For numbers 18–20, use the equation \(2(x−0.1x+2.70)=56.70\).</para>

<!--Q18-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What was done to Noah’s equation to make this one?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
  
  <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      The left side only was multiplied by 2.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q19-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What is the interpretation of this new equation?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
  
  <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      Two pairs of jeans after the 10% discount and sales tax cost 56.70.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q20-->
<exercise>
